From 60ba07509e5fc5981b162f4a293ce8d4b0288ed1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pepopepopepo <311073437+pepopepopepo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 06:22:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: byte-compile lisp/ in make packages, which runs in a
 temporary HOME

lisp/ (19 files) ran interpreted: scripts/build-packages.el compiled
lib/ only, so whatever the byte compiler would have said about the own
modules stayed unseen, and they loaded as source at each start (HANDOFF
2, 10). The compiler now runs over lisp/ after lib/, with init.el's
prelude in force (the generated load path and autoloads, use-package's
settings, the platform defaults file; emacs-cpp-defaults-feature is read
from init.el so the platform mapping keeps one source, D-055), because
four modules expand use-package forms that need it; a warning is an
error, as it is for the tests. Old .elc files are deleted first,
lisp/*.elc is git-ignored, and init-packages-are-built-and-fresh covers
them.

Compiling a module runs its top-level requires, so sibling modules load
and turn their modes on: recentf-mode and savehist-mode wrote
~/.emacs.d/recentf.eld and ~/.emacs.d/history during the first build.
make packages therefore runs in a temporary HOME like make test (D-104),
through one shared helper; REAL_HOME=1 opts out of both.

Everything the compiler reported was fixed in the modules, none of it a
change of behaviour: 33 declarations (declare-function, defvar) of
functions and variables a module uses before loading their package,
docstrings wider than 80 columns rewrapped, and init-cmake.el's
:load-path, which read a defconst of the same file at expansion time
(eval-and-compile).

Checked: make packages (33 packages, 216 lib files, 19 own files, exit
0); make check ok; make test, 133 tests, the same 51 failures as before
the change on a machine without the C++ grammar (D-008), with
init-packages-are-built-and-fresh passing; the real ~/.emacs.d untouched
by the build.

Reasoning:
What was wrong: the own modules were the one part of the tree the byte
  compiler never saw; their warnings were unknown and their load cost
  paid at every start.
Evidence: the first compile over lisp/ reported 44 warnings in 15
  modules (review C-10); four modules do not compile standalone
  (use-package reads the defaults and autoloads at expansion).
Ruled out: compiling lisp/ standalone (the four failures); native-
  compiling lisp/ in the build (Emacs does it on load, into eln-cache);
  silencing the warnings instead of fixing them (seeing them is the
  point); the real HOME for the build (the two state files above).
Tier: 2

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
